--000000000000d3282c065f37ae2c
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"

Hi all,

I'd like to bring KAFKA-21056
<https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-21056> here for broader input.
It proposes replacing gradle/dependencies.gradle with a Gradle version
catalog and enabling Dependabot for Gradle dependencies. I'm not sure it
needs a KIP; if it does, I can draft one.

Motivation
- Upgrades are manual. The ben-manes versions plugin and OWASP
dependency-check are configured but don't run in CI. The only automated CVE
check is docker_scan.yml, which scans images after release.
dependencies.gradle has had 59 commits so far this year.
- Dependabot, Renovate and IntelliJ understand version catalogs but don't
reliably parse our ext map. A catalog would also centralise most plugin
versions, which are currently hardcoded in build.gradle.
- Faster CVE fixes for the libraries shipped like jackson, jetty, log4j,
etc.

Plan
- Migrate to libs.versions.toml, including plugin versions.
- Add a "gradle" entry to .github/dependabot.yml so Dependabot opens PRs,
which can be reviewed manually.
- Group PRs weekly or monthly, and ignore or pin specific libraries (ex:
slf4j, scala).
- Target trunk, and optionally release branches.
- Every upgrade of a shipped library also needs a LICENSE-binary update
(verify_license.py fails CI otherwise). That could likely be automated too.

Dowsides of this approach
- PRs to be reviewed
- sometimes the bumps can be unsafe like slf4j

Looking forward to your feedback.

Thanks,
Murali

