Subject: [jira] [Created] (KAFKA-21410) Log cleaner ignores a committed
 tombstone after an earlier aborted transaction of the same producer; the
 deleted key comes back
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
X-JIRA-FingerPrint: 30527f35849b9dde25b450d4833f0394
From: "Mykyta Bozhenko (Jira) via dev" <dev@kafka.apache.org>
Reply-To: <dev@kafka.apache.org>
Cc: "Mykyta Bozhenko (Jira)" <jira@apache.org>

Mykyta Bozhenko created KAFKA-21410:
---------------------------------------

             Summary: Log cleaner ignores a committed tombstone after an earlier aborted transaction of the same producer; the deleted key comes back
                 Key: KAFKA-21410
                 URL: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-21410
             Project: Kafka
          Issue Type: Bug
          Components: log
    Affects Versions: 4.4.0
         Environment: macOS arm64, OpenJDK 17, single KRaft node; also 1 controller + 3 brokers (RF 3) on Linux (Docker) and OpenBSD 8.0
            Reporter: Mykyta Bozhenko
            Assignee: Mykyta Bozhenko
         Attachments: LogCleanerTest.patch, cluster-observations.txt, fix.patch, repro-output.txt, repro.sh, unit-test-output.txt

On a compacted topic written by transactional producers, the log cleaner can ignore a tombstone (or a newer value) that a producer committed. It keeps the key's older value, removes the tombstone after
delete.retention.ms, and from then on a consumer reading from the beginning gets the deleted key back with its old value. Nothing is logged at WARN or ERROR. Each replica runs its own cleaner; in a run with
three replicas all three ended up with the old value ([^cluster-observations.txt]).

Expected, per the compaction guarantee in the documentation (https://kafka.apache.org/44/design/design/#what-guarantees-does-log-compaction-provide): "Any consumer progressing from the start of the log will
see at least the final state of all records in the order they were written".
Actual: the consumer reads the old value.

It happens when a cleaning pass stops in the middle of a segment because its offset map is full (log.cleaner.dedupe.buffer.size; about 5 million keys per cleaner thread with the default 128 MiB), the same
producer id has an aborted transaction that ended earlier in that segment, before the position where the next pass starts reading, and the next pass reaches a later committed batch of that producer. Typical
case: exactly-once writers such as Kafka Streams changelogs with exactly_once_v2, where aborts follow rebalances, timeouts or crashes.

h3. Reproduction (attached)

* [^repro.sh]: one broker+controller from a Kafka distribution, about 3 minutes. {{KAFKA_HOME=<kafka dir> bash repro.sh p1}} shows the bug; {{p2}} is the control, where a different producer commits the
tombstone. Output on 4.4.0: [^repro-output.txt]. With p1 the consumer returns K=v1 although K was deleted; with p2 it does not return K.
* [^LogCleanerTest.patch]: a unit test for trunk 8aa57006ff. Run {{git apply LogCleanerTest.patch}} and {{./gradlew :storage:test --tests
'org.apache.kafka.storage.internals.log.LogCleanerTest.testCommittedTombstone*'}}. The same-producer case fails, the other-producer case passes: [^unit-test-output.txt].

h3. Cause

Cleaner#buildOffsetMap seeds CleanedTransactionMetadata with log.collectAbortedTransactions(start, end)
([Cleaner.java#L730-L732|https://github.com/apache/kafka/blob/8aa57006ff5da4844776d1047bfee1619faea47f/storage/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/storage/internals/log/Cleaner.java#L730-L732]), and
UnifiedLog#collectAbortedTransactions reads the transaction indexes from the segment at or below start but uses the log start offset, not start, as the lower bound
([UnifiedLog.java#L1676-L1678|https://github.com/apache/kafka/blob/8aa57006ff5da4844776d1047bfee1619faea47f/storage/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/storage/internals/log/UnifiedLog.java#L1676-L1678],
[LocalLog.java#L628-L633|https://github.com/apache/kafka/blob/8aa57006ff5da4844776d1047bfee1619faea47f/storage/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/storage/internals/log/LocalLog.java#L628-L633]). So an aborted
transaction that ended before start is seeded as ongoing. Reading starts at the offset index entry at or below start
([Cleaner.java#L788|https://github.com/apache/kafka/blob/8aa57006ff5da4844776d1047bfee1619faea47f/storage/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/storage/internals/log/Cleaner.java#L788]); if the ABORT marker lies
before that position it is never read, and only an ABORT marker clears the producer
([CleanedTransactionMetadata.java#L83-L155|https://github.com/apache/kafka/blob/8aa57006ff5da4844776d1047bfee1619faea47f/storage/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/storage/internals/log/CleanedTransactionMetada
ta.java#L83-L155]). Every later transactional batch of that producer, committed or not, is then treated as aborted and its keys are left out of the offset map
([Cleaner.java#L809-L814|https://github.com/apache/kafka/blob/8aa57006ff5da4844776d1047bfee1619faea47f/storage/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/storage/internals/log/Cleaner.java#L809-L814]), so the older
value of the key is kept. The lower bound has been the log start offset since KAFKA-5196 (0.11.0.0); KAFKA-13727 fixed the upper bound of the same partial-pass case in Cleaner#cleanSegments.

h3. Proposed fix

[^fix.patch]: in Cleaner#buildOffsetMap, seed only the aborted transactions with lastOffset() >= start. Cleaner#cleanSegments keeps the aborted transactions of the whole segment (it rebuilds the transaction
index of the new segment, see KAFKA-13727), so UnifiedLog#collectAbortedTransactions does not change. With the patch, both new tests and LogCleanerTest, LogCleanerManagerTest and LogCleanerIntegrationTest
pass.



