On Mon, Nov 2, 2026 at 11:05=E2=80=AFPM Nicole Du <nicoleywdu@gmail.com> wr=
ote:

> Hi Rajini,
>
> Thanks for the questions!
>
> For RS01: Done.
>
> For RS02: Yes, MM2 would need to retry without the option to support olde=
r
> brokers. It currently uses valid(), so it could preserve its existing
> handling of partial results by inspecting errors() on the same
> ListGroupsResult. If any error is an UnsupportedVersionException, it woul=
d
> retry once with only requireCommittedOffsets disabled, keeping the other
> filters unchanged. The retry result would replace the initial result. I
> have added this adoption guidance to the KIP and clarified that changes t=
o
> MM2 itself are not proposed here.
>
> For RS03: An existence lookup is sufficient. The committed-offsets map
> removes a group's entry when its last offset is deleted, and offsets of
> uncommitted transactions are stored separately. Checking for the group's
> entry against the same committed state as the group listing gives the
> required semantics without additional state or a per-partition scan.
>
> Best,
> Nicole
>
> On Mon, Nov 2, 2026 at 5:40=E2=80=AFAM Rajini Sivaram <rajinisivaram@gmai=
l.com>
> wrote:
>
> > Hi Nicole,
> >
> > Thanks for the KIP! The KIP looks good, just a few minor comments:
> >
> > RS01: Can we add a link to the KIP in the KIPs under discussion table i=
n
> >
> >
> https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/spaces/KAFKA/pages/50859233/Kafka+Imp=
rovement+Proposals
> > ?
> >
> > RS02: The KIP gives an example of using `all()` instead of `valid()`.
> Since
> > we talk about MirrorMaker in the motivation section, it will be good to
> > include details about how MirrorMaker can be updated to use this KIP. I=
n
> > addition to calling ListGroupsOptions.forConsumerGroups().
> > requireCommittedOffsets(true)).all().get();  , MM2 would also need to
> retry
> > without requireCommittedOffsets on  UnsupportedVersionException?
> >
> > RS03: The KIP says that the filter "*adds a per-group in-memory existen=
ce
> > check*". Is this just a boolean check or does it need anything addition=
al
> > for the semantics described in the KIP?
> >
> > Thanks,
> >
> > Rajini
> >
> > On Wed, Oct 28, 2026 at 4:28=E2=80=AFPM Nicole Du <nicoleywdu@gmail.com=
> wrote:
> >
> > > Hi David,
> > >
> > > Thanks for taking a look and for the suggestions!
> > >
> > > For DJ01: My reasoning was to keep the predicate focused on offsets
> that
> > > have already been committed. Pending transactional offsets may still =
be
> > > aborted, so they do not count on their own. A group with an existing
> > > committed offset still matches even if it also has pending offsets.
> > >
> > > I=E2=80=99ve clarified this in the KIP. One trade-off is that a pendi=
ng-only
> > group
> > > that commits after the listing is evaluated needs a subsequent listin=
g
> to
> > > be discovered. I=E2=80=99d appreciate your thoughts on whether includ=
ing
> > > pending-only groups would be more useful for callers.
> > >
> > > For DJ02: That makes sense. I=E2=80=99ve replaced the snapshot-specif=
ic wording
> > > with a definition based on the coordinator=E2=80=99s committed state =
and
> > simplified
> > > the table to three cases: at least one committed offset, pending
> offsets
> > > only, and neither. A separate sentence clarifies that offsets eligibl=
e
> > for
> > > expiration continue to count until their deletion takes effect.
> > >
> > > I=E2=80=99ve also made two small updates to the existing test plan: e=
xplicitly
> > > covering pending offsets through transaction commit and abort, and
> > > describing the visibility test in terms of committed state.
> > >
> > > Best,
> > > Nicole
> > >
> > > On Wed, Oct 28, 2026 at 7:51=E2=80=AFAM David Jacot <dajac@apache.org=
> wrote:
> > >
> > > > Hi Nicole,
> > > >
> > > > Thanks for the KIP! I have only two comments:
> > > >
> > > > DJ01: What's the reasoning for not including groups with only pendi=
ng
> > > > offsets?
> > > > DJ02: I wonder if we should simplify the "State of the group at the
> > > > read snapshot" table. For instance, "is visible in the snapshot" is
> an
> > > > implementation detail. We should rather say that the condition is
> > > > applied to the committed state and simplify the table.
> > > >
> > > > Best,
> > > > David
> > > >
> > > >
> > > > On Mon, Oct 26, 2026 at 11:49=E2=80=AFPM Nicole Du <nicoleywdu@gmai=
l.com>
> > wrote:
> > > > >
> > > > > Hi all,
> > > > >
> > > > > I'd like to start a discussion on KIP-1388, which proposes adding=
 a
> > > > > request-side filter to the ListGroups API to allow Admin.listGrou=
ps
> > to
> > > > > return only groups with at least one committed offset.
> > > > >
> > > > > Tools that replicate consumer group offsets across clusters, such
> as
> > > > > MirrorMaker 2 and the group offset sync in KIP-1279, typically li=
st
> > all
> > > > > groups before fetching their offsets. In large deployments, many
> > groups
> > > > may
> > > > > have active members but no committed offsets, resulting in
> > unnecessary
> > > > > OffsetFetch requests and additional load on group coordinators.
> > > > >
> > > > > This KIP proposes filtering these groups on the server side by
> > > > introducing
> > > > > a new request field in ListGroups v6 and exposing it through
> > > > > ListGroupsOptions.requireCommittedOffsets(boolean).
> > > > >
> > > > > KIP:
> > > > >
> > > >
> > >
> >
> https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/display/KAFKA/KIP-1388%3A+Add+a+commi=
tted-offsets+filter+to+ListGroups
> > > > >
> > > > > JIRA: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-21366
> > > > >
> > > > > I'd appreciate any feedback or suggestions.
> > > > >
> > > > > Thanks,
> > > > > Nicole
